import hashlib,json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
p=ROOT/'book/solutions/baseline-source90/pages/verification.html.json'
baseline=json.loads(p.read_bytes());replacements={};reasons={}
def add(key,reason,body):replacements[key]=body.strip()+'\n';reasons[key]=reason
add('book-m15-b1','Derives both activation branches, their endpoint extrema and the exact temperature specification, including equality and domain change.',r'''
<p><strong>1. Determine which ReLUs can change status.</strong> The correction is $n(z)=\operatorname{ReLU}(z+0.2)-2\operatorname{ReLU}(z-0.1)$. On the new interval $[-0.1,0.5]$, $z+0.2\ge0.1\gt0$, so the first unit is active everywhere. The second changes status only at $z=0.1$.</p>
<p><strong>2. Optimize each affine branch.</strong> For $-0.1\le z\le0.1$, the second output is zero and $n(z)=z+0.2$, increasing from $0.1$ to $0.3$ degrees. For $0.1\le z\le0.5$, both units are active and $n(z)=z+0.2-2(z-0.1)=0.4-z$, decreasing from $0.3$ to $-0.1$ degrees. The maximum $0.3$ is attained at $z=0.1$, and the minimum $-0.1$ at $z=0.5$. The two continuous branches therefore give exact range $[-0.1,0.3]$.</p>
<p><strong>3. Apply the specified upper limit.</strong> The worst modeled temperature is $59.7+0.3=60.0$ degrees, so every allowed feature satisfies the stated non-strict $\le60$ limit. The certificate has zero margin. A strict-separation requirement or an additional positive implementation/model error would need a new allowance. The old correction minimum zero cannot be reused after the input domain changes, and the additional calibrated residual was explicitly excluded from this exercise.</p>
''')
add('book-m15-b2','Solves the ceiling/rank availability condition, explains the single-trajectory event and carries it into every temperature inequality with the correct marginal scope.',r'''
<p><strong>1. Find when a finite order statistic is available.</strong> The calibration index is $k=\lceil0.99(n+1)\rceil$. Because $n$ is an integer, $k\le n$ holds exactly when $0.99(n+1)\le n$. Rearranging gives $0.99\le0.01n$, hence $n\ge99$. At the smallest size $n=99$, $k=\lceil99\rceil=99$, the largest calibration trajectory score. Smaller samples require the conservative infinite-threshold convention.</p>
<p><strong>2. Translate the score event into a joint residual bound.</strong> Each calibration example is a complete ten-step trajectory, scored by $s=\max_{1\le t\le10}|r_t|$. Thus $s\le0.32$ is exactly the event that every one of the ten residual magnitudes is at most $0.32$ degrees. Under exchangeability of the $99$ calibration trajectories together with the next trajectory, split conformal gives this one event marginal probability at least $99/100=0.99$.</p>
<p><strong>3. Test all ten temperatures on that event.</strong> At each step the nominal value is $59.35$, the neural correction is at most $0.3$, and the residual is at most $0.32$. Therefore $T_t\le59.35+0.3+0.32=59.97\le60$ degrees for every $t=1,\ldots,10$, leaving $0.03$ degrees of margin. The proposal passes the modeled inequality throughout the forecast on the joint residual-coverage event.</p>
<p>This is marginal coverage over calibration and new-trajectory randomness, not a deterministic promise about every future trajectory or a conditional guarantee at every selected input. Ten separate 99 percent pointwise statements would, by a union bound alone, give only $1-10(0.01)=0.90$ joint coverage. Scoring the whole trajectory directly changes the calibration object and supplies the requested joint event.</p>
''')
add('practice-medium-4','Derives the Hoeffding sample-count inequality, checks the integer rounding and shows why the desired population target cannot be attained at this tolerance.',r'''
<p><strong>1. Solve the one-sided failure-probability bound.</strong> For a fixed controller and fresh i.i.d. bounded success indicators, Hoeffding gives $\Pr(p\lt\hat\phi-t)\le e^{-2Mt^2}$. To make this at most $0.01$, require $e^{-2M(0.1)^2}\le0.01$. Taking natural logarithms gives $-0.02M\le-\log100$. Multiplication by $-1$ reverses the inequality, so $M\ge\log100/0.02\approx230.25851$. The sufficient integer sample size is $231$.</p>
<p><strong>2. Apply the confidence event to the observed fraction.</strong> If $\hat\phi=0.98$, then on the event supplied by the theorem the true population success probability obeys $p\ge\hat\phi-t=0.98-0.1=0.88$. This lower-confidence procedure covers with probability at least $0.99$ over the independent trials; the observed fraction itself is not the guaranteed population probability.</p>
<p><strong>3. Test the target before collecting more data at the same tolerance.</strong> A lower bound of $0.95$ with $t=0.1$ would require $\hat\phi-0.1\ge0.95$, or $\hat\phi\ge1.05$. A success fraction cannot exceed one, so this tolerance cannot certify that target even if every trial succeeds. A smaller tolerance, such as $0.03$ for the stated fraction, requires a correspondingly larger sample count. Validation must remain independent of controller selection unless the selection effect is explicitly accounted for.</p>
''')
add('practice-hard-3','Expands both binomial tails numerically and proves their strict decrease to justify the smallest admissible count, then explains the two probability levels.',r'''
<p><strong>1. Evaluate the convenient explicit count.</strong> With $\varepsilon=0.1$, $\beta=0.05$ and $d=3$, the rule gives $N\ge(2/0.1)(\log20+3)=20(\log20+3)\approx119.91465$. Rounding up gives $N=120$.</p>
<p><strong>2. Evaluate the exact scenario condition.</strong> Its binomial tail is $S_N=\sum_{i=0}^{2}\binom Ni(0.1)^i(0.9)^{N-i}$. At $N=60$, $\binom{60}{2}=60(59)/2=1770$. Factoring out $0.9^{58}$ gives $S_{60}=0.9^{58}[0.9^2+60(0.1)(0.9)+1770(0.1)^2]=23.91(0.9^{58})\approx0.05304508\gt0.05$.</p>
<p>At $N=61$, $\binom{61}{2}=1830$, so $S_{61}=0.9^{59}[0.81+5.49+18.30]=24.60(0.9^{59})\approx0.04911828\le0.05$. Thus $61$ meets the exact condition while $60$ does not.</p>
<p><strong>3. Justify the minimality comparison.</strong> If $X_N$ is binomial with success probability $0.1$, it can be coupled with $X_{N+1}=X_N+B$, where $B$ is an independent Bernoulli($0.1$) variable. Then $S_{N+1}=S_N-0.1\Pr(X_N=2)$, which is strictly smaller for $N\ge2$. Therefore no earlier count can pass once $60$ fails, and $61$ is the smallest exact admissible count. The larger explicit $120$ is sufficient but conservative.</p>
<p><strong>4. Keep the design and deployment probabilities separate.</strong> With probability at least $1-\beta=0.95$ over the sampled design constraints, the returned solution has population violation probability at most $\varepsilon=0.1$. This does not assert zero violations for every uncertainty realization. Convexity, the theorem's feasibility/solution assumptions and matching i.i.d. deployment sampling remain required.</p>
''')
add('::exercise-16','Shows both neuron-status computations, every signed CROWN coefficient, the piecewise alpha maximization and branchwise exact extrema, preserving all requested comparisons.',r'''
<p><strong>Set up the walkthrough's network.</strong> It has $z_1=x_1+x_2+1$, $z_2=-x_1+2x_2+1$ and $f=-2\operatorname{ReLU}(z_1)+2\operatorname{ReLU}(z_2)$. At center $(1,1)$, the pre-activations are $(3,2)$. An infinity-norm radius $\varepsilon$ gives radii $(2\varepsilon,3\varepsilon)$ by the absolute row sums.</p>
<p><strong>(a) Radius $0.5$: both units are active.</strong> The input box is $[0.5,1.5]^2$ and the pre-activation intervals are $[3-1,3+1]=[2,4]$ and $[2-1.5,2+1.5]=[0.5,3.5]$. Both lower endpoints are positive. IBP treats hidden outputs independently and gives lower value $-2(4)+2(0.5)=-7$ and upper value $-2(2)+2(3.5)=3$.</p>
<p>CROWN preserves the shared input: the exact affine function is $f=-2z_1+2z_2=-4x_1+2x_2$. Its center value is $-2$ and its radius is $0.5(|-4|+|2|)=3$, giving $[-5,1]$. There is no unstable-unit relaxation to optimize, so adaptive CROWN and alpha-CROWN give this same interval. The minimum is attained at $(1.5,0.5)$ and maximum at $(0.5,1.5)$, proving that the interval is exact. IBP's extra width comes entirely from discarding hidden-output dependence.</p>
<p><strong>(b) Radius $1.5$: one unit is unstable.</strong> The input box is $[-0.5,2.5]^2$. The intervals are $z_1\in[0,6]$ and $z_2\in[-2.5,6.5]$. The first ReLU is active including its zero boundary; the second is unstable. IBP gives hidden ranges $[0,6]$ and $[0,6.5]$, so $f\in[-12,13]$.</p>
<p><strong>Derive the lower relaxation.</strong> For the positive output coefficient use the valid lower line $\operatorname{ReLU}(z_2)\ge\alpha z_2$, $0\le\alpha\le1$. Thus $f\ge(-2-2\alpha)x_1+(-2+4\alpha)x_2-2+2\alpha$. Its center value is $-6+4\alpha$; minimizing over the box subtracts $1.5$ times its coefficient 1-norm:</p>
<div class="math-block">$$\gamma^L(\alpha)=-6+4\alpha-1.5[2+2\alpha+|4\alpha-2|]=-9+\alpha-1.5|4\alpha-2|.$$</div>
<p>Adaptive CROWN chooses $\alpha=1$ because $u_2=6.5\ge|l_2|=2.5$, giving $\gamma^L(1)=-11$. To optimize alpha, split the absolute value. On $[0,1/2]$ the expression is $-12+7\alpha$, increasing; on $[1/2,1]$ it is $-6-5\alpha$, decreasing. Its maximum is therefore at $\alpha=1/2$, with value $-8.5$. This proves the alpha-CROWN lower bound.</p>
<p><strong>Derive the upper relaxation.</strong> The upper chord of the second ReLU is $h_U(z_2)=[6.5/(6.5+2.5)](z_2+2.5)=(13/18)(z_2+2.5)$. Because its output coefficient is positive, $f\le-2z_1+2h_U(z_2)$. Expansion yields</p>
<div class="math-block">$$f\le-\frac{31}{9}x_1+\frac89x_2+\frac{55}{18}.$$</div>
<p>This affine upper bound has center value $1/2$ and coefficient 1-norm $(31+8)/9=13/3$. Its box maximum is $1/2+1.5(13/3)=7$. Both CROWN variants use this upper bound here.</p>
<p><strong>Verify the exact range without a relaxation.</strong> On the branch $z_2\ge0$, $f=-4x_1+2x_2$. Since $x_2\ge(x_1-1)/2$, $f\ge-3x_1-1\ge-8.5$, attained at the kink point $(2.5,0.75)$. On $z_2\le0$, $f=-2x_1-2x_2-2$ and $x_2\le(x_1-1)/2$, so $x_1+x_2\le(3x_1-1)/2\le3.25$ and again $f\ge-8.5$. The input $(-0.5,2.5)$ has $z_1=3$, $z_2=6.5$ and $f=-6+13=7$, attaining the sound upper bound. Thus the exact range is $[-8.5,7]$.</p>
<p>Panel A's comparisons are consequently: at radius $0.5$, IBP $[-7,3]$ and all affine/CROWN/exact bounds $[-5,1]$; at radius $1.5$, IBP $[-12,13]$, adaptive CROWN $[-11,7]$, alpha-CROWN and exact $[-8.5,7]$. The derivation identifies both the discarded correlations and the lower-line choice that cause the differences.</p>
''')
add('::exercise-18','Works the Gaussian symmetry and both radius substitutions, derives the all-success binomial confidence inversion and states exact versus rounded caps and strict-ball scope.',r'''
<p><strong>(a) Apply the CERTIFY convention.</strong> With lower bound $\underline p_A=0.95$, CERTIFY uses $\overline p_B=1-\underline p_A=0.05$. Standard-normal symmetry gives $\Phi^{-1}(0.05)=-\Phi^{-1}(0.95)$. Thus</p>
<div class="math-block">$$R=\frac{0.5}{2}[\Phi^{-1}(0.95)-\Phi^{-1}(0.05)]=0.5\Phi^{-1}(0.95)\approx0.5(1.645)=0.8225.$$</div>
<p>The last number uses the supplied rounded quantile; a radius $0.82$ is a conservative rounded choice. On the $99.9$ percent confidence event for the probability estimate, the ideal smoothed classifier keeps its label for every perturbation with $\|\delta\|_2\lt R$. The probability is over CERTIFY's Monte-Carlo samples, not over input perturbations.</p>
<p><strong>(b) Use the additional runner-up information.</strong> If $\overline p_B=0.03$ is valid on the same confidence event, then $\Phi^{-1}(0.03)=-\Phi^{-1}(0.97)$. The supplied values give $R=(0.5/2)(1.645+1.881)=0.25(3.526)=0.8815$, approximately $0.88$. A separately estimated runner-up bound needs joint confidence accounting; it cannot automatically share the first estimate's failure budget.</p>
<p><strong>(c) Derive the finite-sample ceiling.</strong> The most favorable count is that all $n$ Monte-Carlo samples return class $A$. At a proposed true success probability $p$, this outcome has binomial probability $p^n$. Inverting the one-sided tail at level $\alpha$ gives the Clopper–Pearson lower bound $\underline p_A=\alpha^{1/n}$. Therefore the exact maximum formula is</p>
<div class="math-block">$$R_{\max}=0.5\Phi^{-1}(0.001^{1/10000}).$$</div>
<p>Here $0.001^{1/10000}=\exp[-\log1000/10000]\approx0.999309463$. Using the supplied approximate quantile $3.20$ gives a displayed cap about $1.60$. This is an approximation to the exact formula, not a license to round a certified radius upward.</p>
<p>Even unanimous finite samples leave a probability lower bound below one. Moreover $1-\alpha^{1/n}\approx\log(1/\alpha)/n$ for large $n$, while high Gaussian quantiles grow only as the square root of a logarithm. More forward passes therefore increase the cap slowly; the count limits a statistical certificate rather than the classifier's true robustness.</p>
''')
